response:
Yes, loops are used to repeat a certain action a certain number of times.  For example, you might want to print a message to the screen every time the program runs, or you might want to display a message every time the user enters a certain number.  To do this, you can use a loop.  In C, a loop is written like this:

for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    doSomething();
}

The "for" keyword tells the compiler that it should create a loop, and the "int i = 0;" part tells the compiler how many times to repeat the "doSomething()" function.  The "i < 5;" part tells the compiler that the loop should run only up to five times.  The "doSomething()" function is the function that you want to execute inside the loop.  You can call any function inside the loop, and it will be executed for each iteration of the loop.

